    We can use Bayesian inference in order to approximate the values of a given dynamic system.
For dimensions greater than 3, the posterior distribution, P(Theta|X), is not easily found or numerically solved.
To approximate the posterior distribution, we use Markov Chain Monte Carlo methods to continuously update the parameters of the model through previous sampling.
MCMC methods rely on an iterative algorithm to approximate sampling from distributions is similar in application to 
  using Bayesian inference to update our beliefs using prior data 
For our purposes, we will use the Griddy-Gibbs algrorithm.
The Markov Chain acts as an algorithm to asymptotically approach the true parameters by randomly walking through parameter space.
The chain itself is comprised of a finite number of proposed models for our data.



